WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.380
So the thing about SFI is that it's
grassroots, it's bottom-up, it tends to

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.589
work on messy problems,&nbsp;things that are maybe in the sort of

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:10.559
more conceptual or philosophical realm,
and turn them into things that can be

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:12.540
you know, mathematically addressed

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:16.500
and it does that with incredible rigor.
And this is unique to SFI

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.029
in a way – I mean that sort of comfort with
these messy problems and working to make

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:25.019
them rigorous and, you know, amenable to
science. But at the same time it's open

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:29.460
minded and so it embraces a variety of
styles and not just science styles but

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:34.230
you know high standards in art, in the
community, always looking for new ways of

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:37.739
thinking. You know I love the fact that
we have like all of human knowledge in

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:41.969
our pocket now – the iPhone –&nbsp;and I mean,
there's a lot of there's a lot of

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:46.680
negative things about that,
right? We're all glued to our phones. But

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.579
that cumulative cultural knowledge and
everyone having access to it, in

00:00:50.579 --> 00:00:56.280
principle, seems to be huge. And then on
top about having a curious mindset. Those

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.109
would be the things that I would say are
totally essential to thriving.

00:01:01.109 --> 00:01:04.710
I think that's right –&nbsp;I mean, knowledge is the
gift that keeps on giving. Knowledge is

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.439
what would allow us to rebuild
everything if it disappeared tomorrow.

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:13.950
And also I think that knowledge can take
the form of ethical knowledge – so as we

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.869
talked about on the panel, and I'm sort
of obsessed with, we kind of know that

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:23.130
humans are capable of doing really
disturbing things. Almost any of us, put

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.119
in the wrong circumstances. And so we
ought to prioritize, before almost

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:30.900
everything else, making sure that we
don't find ourselves in those

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.710
predicaments that cause us to do
incredibly destructive things that we're capable of doing.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.890
And I think
InterPlanetary gets that discussion

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.640
going, and I also like it because it's
very grassroots, it's very bottom-up, and

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.450
one of the things we didn't get to talk
about in the panel is whether

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.590
globally we should have a
conversation in town halls around

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:54.860
the world about how to address these issues of social

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.880
engineering, creative solutions to social
engineering...you know, whether we

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.750
should be doing it, what kinds of data we
should be using, and in a way that's not

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:06.590
just to make people happy or make them
feel involved but actually because

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.429
there's a lot of data now coming up it
says crowdsourcing, the wisdom of crowds,

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:15.050
that's the way you find the cool new
ideas. It's actually in the collective.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.770
And so, you know, we can think
that it's going to come from scientists

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:24.770
or highly educated policymakers
but we need to be pluralistic, I think,

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.550
and really embrace that community
discussion. Because I think that's where

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.550
we're gonna find the hidden stuff. I
would just +1 everything Jessica said.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:38.660
For me as a non-scientist and a fan of
what happens at the Santa Fe Institute,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:44.480
I think what's so great about this is it
represents not only the idiosyncrasy of

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:48.860
the approach that SFI represents – of
bringing people together from different

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.580
disciplinary backgrounds asking about
the most fundamental questions in the

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.810
universe, but also about really
interesting practical tangible questions

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:02.840
but then it does it in a way that is
less cloistered than almost any academic

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.160
enterprise that I've ever witnessed. So
you both have folks approaching

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.720
knowledge in an interesting and novel
way, and also doing it with a respect for

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:15.500
the fact that there needs to be
engagement with everyday people, with

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:21.799
artists, with crazy people, with random
craftspeople who live in Santa Fe, and

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:26.209
you know, part of what needs to happen,
part of what draws me to this stuff as a

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:32.600
musician, originally, is that what we
really want is for popular culture to be

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.459
scientific culture. And we don't want
there to be this sort of you know priest

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:41.959
class of scientists, and then everyone
else who's mystified by what they do.

00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:47.450
We want everyone to be a scientist and it's
only then that we can collectively make

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:50.890
the best of our capabilities.

